# DRDS Website V2 — PRODUCTION host configuration for drdigitalsystems.co.za
#
# This file belongs in the apex document root, alongside the Astro static build.
# It is tracked here, in the repository, because it is production configuration
# and not a build artefact: nothing in `website/` emits it, `npm run build` never
# produces it, and a deploy that forgets it is a deploy that silently loses every
# canonical redirect below.
#
# It is deliberately NOT placed inside `website/public/`. Anything in `public/`
# is copied verbatim into EVERY build, so a staging upload would carry this file
# to v2.drdigitalsystems.co.za and overwrite the cPanel-managed .htaccess that
# host relies on for its Directory Privacy. Staging must keep its own. This file
# is copied into the apex document root by hand, once, at cutover.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT
#
# Website V2 is a static site. Every route is a plain HTML file already sitting
# at the path it is served from, so there is no application rewrite to write and
# none is written here. This file does exactly four things:
#
#   1. refuses directory listings;
#   2. names index.html as the directory index;
#   3. canonicalises every request onto https://drdigitalsystems.co.za;
#   4. redirects the nine legacy WordPress URLs that Website V2 absorbs.
#
# It contains NO WordPress rewrite block, NO PHP handler, NO reverse proxy, NO
# rewrite for /api/ or the Growth Snapshot (the browser calls Railway directly,
# cross-origin by design — see the CORS note in README.md beside this file), and
# NO caching, compression or other speculative performance directives. Those are
# not free: each one is a behaviour someone has to debug later, and none of them
# is needed to serve four files correctly.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOP BEHAVIOUR — stated accurately, because "one hop" is easy to claim and easy
# to get wrong.
#
# The legacy redirects are listed BEFORE the origin canonicalisation and target
# ABSOLUTE canonical URLs. That ordering is the whole reason the worst case is
# one hop rather than two: a request for http://www.drdigitalsystems.co.za/growth-audit/
# is rewritten straight to https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/ instead of
# being canonicalised to the apex first and redirected again afterwards.
#
#   http://drdigitalsystems.co.za/              -> 1 hop  -> https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/
#   https://www.drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/-> 1 hop  -> https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/
#   http://www.drdigitalsystems.co.za/          -> 1 hop  -> https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/
#   https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/growth-audit/-> 1 hop  -> https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/
#   http://www.drdigitalsystems.co.za/growth-audit/ -> 1 hop -> https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/
#   https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/    -> 0 hops -> 200
#
# No rule can loop: every legacy target (/, /snapshot/, /start/) is itself an
# already-canonical https apex URL and is never a legacy SOURCE, so the second
# request matches nothing and is served from disk.

# Never expose a directory listing. The build emits /_astro/ full of hashed
# assets; that directory has an obvious URL and no index file.
#
# If the host ever answers 500 after this file is placed, THIS is the first line
# to suspect: it requires AllowOverride Options, which cPanel grants by default
# but a hardened vhost may not.
Options -Indexes

# Astro is built with `build.format: "directory"`, so /snapshot/ is really
# /snapshot/index.html. Stated explicitly rather than inherited: the previous
# occupant of this document root was PHP, and the server default may still list
# index.php ahead of index.html.
DirectoryIndex index.html

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # ── 1. Legacy WordPress URLs that Website V2 absorbs ──────────────────
  #
  # Ratified migration map. Each target is the surface that now does the job,
  # not a rebuilt copy of the old page:
  #
  #   /growth-audit/  the Complimentary Growth Audit is the Growth Snapshot now
  #   /strategy-call/ the deprecated discovery call; /start/ owns this funnel
  #   /services/      the homepage ladder states the offer better
  #   /method/        doctrine; preserved as reference material, not rebuilt
  #   /framework/     doctrine; preserved as reference material, not rebuilt
  #   /about/         the entity statement now lives in the site footer
  #   /case-studies/ + the "Coming Q3 2026" post + its category archive —
  #                   an unfulfilled promise that is retired on purpose
  #
  # 301 rather than 410 throughout: none of this is harmful-if-found, and a
  # permanent redirect keeps whatever link equity these URLs hold.
  #
  # WordPress plumbing (/wp-admin/, /wp-login.php, /wp-json/, /feed/, /author/…,
  # /?s=, /xmlrpc.php) is deliberately absent. It disappears with WordPress and
  # 404s honestly. Redirecting it would be pretending it moved somewhere.
  #
  # The optional trailing slash in each pattern catches the unslashed variant
  # without a second rule or a second redirect.
  RewriteRule ^growth-audit/?$              https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/snapshot/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^strategy-call/?$             https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/start/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^services/?$                  https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^method/?$                    https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^framework/?$                 https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^about/?$                     https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^case-studies/?$              https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^case-studies-coming-q3-2026/?$ https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^category/announcements/?$    https://drdigitalsystems.co.za/ [R=301,L]

  # ── 2. One canonical origin: https://drdigitalsystems.co.za ───────────
  #
  # HTTPS and www are handled by a single rule with an [OR] condition, so a
  # request that is wrong on both counts is corrected in ONE redirect.
  #
  # HTTPS is enforced explicitly rather than inherited. The pre-cutover site
  # did redirect http to https, but that redirect's configuration source was
  # never proven — it may live in the vhost, or it may have lived in the
  # WordPress .htaccess this file replaces. Relying on it would be relying on
  # something we cannot see.
  #
  # www is not, and must not become, a second live frontend origin. The Snapshot
  # API's CORS allowlist is exact-origin and will contain the apex only, so a
  # visitor served the site from www would get a page whose Growth Snapshot and
  # Growth Report enquiry are both refused by the browser. This redirect is what
  # guarantees no application JavaScript ever executes on the www host.
  #
  # %{REQUEST_URI} preserves the full path; mod_rewrite reattaches the query
  # string automatically because the substitution contains no "?" of its own.
  RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on [OR]
  RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\. [NC]
  RewriteRule ^ https://drdigitalsystems.co.za%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]
</IfModule>
